ines total. So each line must intersect column 1. That means each line must pass through a distinct point in column 1. So we cannot have any vertical line x=1 (which would intersect column 1 at all 5 points). So we cannot use vertical line x=1. So all 5 lines must intersect column 1 at exactly one point each. That means we need to assign each point in column 1 to a distinct line. Since we have 2 sunny lines, they can each cover one point in column 1. The remaining 3 points in column 1 must be covered by the 3 non-sunny lines, each intersecting column 1 at exactly one point. So the non-sunny lines cannot be vertical (since vertical would intersect column 1 at all points). So they must be horizontal or diagonal lines.\n\nNow consider column 2 (size 4). Each line can intersect column 2 at most one point. So we have 5 lines, each can intersect column 2 at at most one point, so we can cover at most 5 points in column 2, which is enough (need 4). So column 2 is okay.\n\nSimilarly column 3 (size 3) and column 4 (size 2) and column 5 (size 1) are okay.\n\nThus the main difficulty is to assign lines such that each line passes through a distinct point in column 1, and also covers other points in other columns appropriately.\n\nWe need to find 5 lines (2 sunny, 3 non-sunny) such that each line passes through a distinct point (a,1) for a=1,...,5? Actually column 1 points are (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). So we need each line to pass through one of these points.\n\nWe can try to construct: Use sunny line L1 through (1,1) and (5,1) maybe? That's horizontal, not sunny. So not allowed. Use sunny line L1 through (1,1) and (2,3) maybe? Slope = (3-1)/(2-1)=2, sunny. L2 through (1,2) and (3,4) slope = (4-2)/(3-1)=1, sunny? slope 1 is sunny (since not 0, ∞, -1). So L2 is sunny. Then we need three non-sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) that each intersect column 1 at (1,3),(1,4),(1,5). But they cannot be vertical (since vertical would intersect column 1 at all points). So they