se a vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines; each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. Since we have only k=2 sunny lines, we could assign each sunny line to one of the points in column n-1. That's possible. Then they consider point (n,1). They claim that a line joining (n,1) with either of the two points of column n-1 is either horizontal (slope 0) or slope -1, both non-sunny. Let's verify.\n\nColumn n-1 points are (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). The point (n,1) is to the right of both. The line through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (y=1), slope 0, non-sunny. The line through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1, non-sunny. So indeed any line connecting (n,1) to a point in column n-1 is non-sunny. So (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line that also covers a point in column n-1. Since we have only two sunny lines, and they are already used to cover the two points in column n-1, we cannot use a sunny line to cover (n,1). So (n,1) must be covered by a non-sunny line. But we have already used a vertical line at column n-2, and we have n-2 non-sunny lines total. For n≥3, n-2≥1. So we have at least one non-sunny line left (since we used one for x=n-2). Could we use a non-sunny line to cover (n,1)? Yes, we could use a horizontal line y=1 (covers (n,1) and also (n-2,1) maybe). But (n-2,1) is already covered by vertical line x=n-2. That's okay. So we could use a horizontal line y=1 as a non-sunny line to cover (n,1). However, we need to ensure that we have enough non-sunny lines to cover all other points. Let's see.\n\nWe have n-2 non-sunny lines total. One is vertical x=n-2. We need to cover column n-1's two points with sunny lines (since we assume we don't use vertical line x=n-1). So we have used both sunny lines for those points. Then we need to cover (n,1) with a non-sunny line, maybe horizontal y=1. That uses a second non-sunny line. So far we have used 2 non-sunny lines: vertical x=n-2 and horizontal y=1. We have n-2 to