ave exactly the same number k of sunny lines.\" This seems plausible: If we have a non-sunny line that is horizontal or slope -1, it can cover at most one point in each column (i.e., for each x coordinate). So if we replace it by a vertical line at a column that hasn't been covered by any vertical line yet, we will cover at least as many points (maybe more) because vertical line covers all points in that column. However, we need to ensure that the new vertical line does not coincide with any existing line (i.e., distinctness). Since we choose leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line, we guarantee distinctness. Also, we need to ensure that the new vertical line does not become sunny (i.e., vertical lines are non-sunny). Indeed vertical lines are parallel to y-axis, so they are non-sunny. So the replacement is valid. So we can assume non-sunny lines are vertical.\n\nThus we have n-k vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-k (or some set of n-k distinct vertical lines). The points with x ≤ n-k are covered. The remaining points are those with x > n-k, i.e., x from n-k+1 to n (since a+b ≤ n+1). But the solution defines T_k = { (x,y) ∈ Z_{>0}^2 | x+y ≤ k+1 }. Wait, why k+1? Let's check: The original S_n = { (a,b) ∈ Z_{>0}^2 | a+b ≤ n+1 }. If we have vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-k, then points with a ≤ n-k are covered. The remaining points have a > n-k. Let a' = a - (n-k). Then a' ≥ 1. Also a+b ≤ n+1 => (a' + (n-k)) + b ≤ n+1 => a' + b ≤ k+1. So the remaining points are { (a',b) | a' ≥ 1, b ≥ 1, a'+b ≤ k+1 }. That's exactly T_k. So the reduction is correct.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering T_k with exactly k sunny lines (since we have k sunny lines left to cover the remaining points). The vertical lines are non-sunny and already placed. So we need to cover T_k with k sunny lines. The solution then gives a bound: each sunny line can contain at most floor((k+1)/2) points of T_k. Then they count: total points in T_k = k(k+1)/2. So k * floor((k+1)/2) ≥ k(k+1)/2 => floor((k+