lem reduces to covering T_k with k sunny lines (since we have k sunny lines left). The vertical lines are non-sunny and already placed.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: For which k does there exist a set of k distinct lines (all sunny) covering all integer points in the triangle T_k = {(x,y)∈Z_{>0}^2 : x+y ≤ k+1}? The lines must be distinct and not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1. So they cannot be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. So they must have slopes other than 0, ∞, -1.\n\nWe need to find all k for which we can cover T_k with k such lines.\n\nThe solution then proceeds to bound the number of points a sunny line can cover in T_k. They claim that any sunny line can cover at most floor((k+1)/2) points in T_k. They derive this bound using the representation p x - q y = c with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q. Then the integer points on the line are (x0+qt, y0+pt). The sum x+y = (x0+y0)+(p+q)t. Since p+q ≠ 0 (sunny condition), the sum changes by at least 1 per step. The sum must stay between 2 and k+1 inclusive. So the number of integer t values is at most floor((k+1-2)/|p+q|)+1 = floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). They also consider the case |p+q|=1 and argue that the bound still holds.\n\nThus each sunny line can cover at most floor((k+1)/2) points in T_k.\n\nNow we have k sunny lines, each covering at most floor((k+1)/2) points. The total number of points in T_k is k(k+1)/2. So we need k * floor((k+1)/2) ≥ k(k+1)/2. This simplifies to floor((k+1)/2) ≥ (k+1)/2. Since floor(x) ≤ x, equality holds only if (k+1)/2 is integer, i.e., k odd. For even k, floor((k+1)/2) = k/2, which is less than (k+1)/2, so the inequality fails. So even k cannot work (except maybe k=0? Let's check). For k=0, T_0 is empty? Actually T_0 = {(x,y) : x+y ≤ 1} with x,y positive integers => no points. So k=0 works trivially: we have no sunny lines needed. So k=0 is allowed. For k=1, T_1 = {(1,1)} (since x+y ≤ 2). So we need 1 sunny line covering that point. Any line n